{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to write drafts as uncommitted, unstaged edits to the book with no draft marker, adding a new chapter file to `SUMMARY.md` and recording it with `git add -N`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The text of `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.drafts` says only what the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to do. Agents write drafts as uncommitted, unstaged book edits with no draft marker. A new chapter file goes into `SUMMARY.md` and is recorded with `git add -N`. The text names no library, algorithm, signature, type, field or delegation through a named interface. The only artifact is the skill's content, and the clause binds what that content says, not how it is built. The skill could be reorganized into different sections or reworded, and it would still satisfy the clause if it gave the same direction. The one structural point is that the direction has to appear in the skill. The review root `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` is already the implementing source, and `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.drafts` already has an Implements edge to it. So the clause adds no structural obligation that needs further implementation evidence. I did not check whether the skill text actually gives this direction. That is a content check, not a structural one.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement obliges the `habeas-requirements` skill to direct agents to do three things. They write drafts as uncommitted, unstaged edits. They use no draft marker. They add a new chapter to `SUMMARY.md` and record it with `git add -N`.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_writes_drafts_as_unstaged_edits_without_markers]` covers all three. It locates the numbered workflow step containing \"Write drafts\" in the bundled `habeas-requirements` skill, using `step_of`, `step` and `workflow_steps`. `workflow_steps` collapses wrapped continuation lines. The test then calls `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` for each phrase.\n\n- The phrase \"Write drafts from the request straight into the chapter as uncommitted, unstaged edits, with no draft marker\" covers the uncommitted, unstaged and no-marker obligations.\n- The phrase \"For a new chapter, add it to `SUMMARY.md` and `git add -N` it\" covers the new-chapter obligation.\n\nThe bundled skill text in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` (Workflow, step 2) contains both phrases once its wrapped lines are collapsed. The test would fail if either instruction were removed or reworded.\n\nThe requirement is about skill text, and substring assertions on the right step are the appropriate check. I found no other behavioral obligation, boundary or failure case that needs a separate test.",
